>> title: New England Boiled Dinner
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, corned beef, cabbage, one-pot, simmer, dinner
>> counters: One Pot
>> aka: boiled dinner, corned beef and cabbage, Yankee boiled dinner, corned beef dinner
>> servings: 6
>> time: 4 hr
>> slack: narrow — a rolling boil squeezes the brisket into string that ten degrees lower would have made tender
>> washing-up: the stockpot

>> step: The beef takes three hours, the roots twenty minutes, the cabbage fifteen. They go in backwards from the end.
One pot, and the whole discipline is the order things go into it.

>> step: simmer 3 hr, barely moving
Simmer @corned beef brisket{4%lb}(1.8 kg; rinsed) in a #stockpot{} for ~simmer{3%hr} with @water{4%qt}(3.8 L; to cover), @bay leaves{2}, @black peppercorns{1%tsp}, @yellow onion{1%large}(halved).

>> step: simmer the roots 20 min
Add @Yukon Gold potatoes{2%lb}(900 g; halved), @carrots{6%medium}(cut in 2-in lengths), @turnip{1%medium}(peeled and wedged) to @&(~1)pot{} and ~simmer{20%min}.

>> step: simmer the cabbage 15 min
Add @green cabbage{1%small}(cut in 6 wedges through the core) to @&(~1)pot{} and ~simmer{15%min}, until a knife goes through the stem.

>> step: rest 15 min, carve across the grain
Lift @&(~1)boiled dinner{} out with @flat-leaf parsley{1/4%cup}(chopped), @grainy mustard{2%Tbs}, and ~rest{15%min} before carving the brisket across the grain.

>> step: Keep the pot at a shiver. Ladle some over the sliced meat and keep the rest for soup.
Keep the pot at a shiver, never a boil. Ladle a little of it over the sliced meat, and keep the rest for soup.
